he dish is prepared from live sand worms, which are put in hot water to remove their tentacles, and then mixed with raw egg. Onions, mandarin peels and various spices are added, and the mixture is then fried until it obtains a crispy brown surface."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cha-ruoi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%E1%BA%A3_r%C6%B0%C6%A1i"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Tylorrhynchus heterochetus","ingredient/tylorrhynchus-heterochetus"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","ref":"site/vietworldkitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's blog on Vietnamese cooking, ingredients and technique.","url":"https://vietworldkitchen.com/blog/category/recipes-vietnamese","suggested":true},{"name":"Hungry Huy","ref":"site/hungryhuy-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hungryhuy.com","note":"Vietnamese-American Huy Vu's recipes for home-style Vietnamese dishes.","url":"https://www.hungryhuy.com/vietnamese/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Vietnamese cuisine","links":[["Alkemē","dish/alkeme"],["Bánh bao","dish/banh-bao"],["Bánh bột lọc","dish/banh-bot-loc"],["Bánh giầy","dish/banh-giay"],["Bánh tráng","dish/banh-trang"],["Chả trứng","dish/cha-trung"],["Chạo tôm","dish/chao-tom"],["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"],["Cốm","dish/com"],["Ha VL","dish/ha-vl"],["Hanoi Kitchen","dish/hanoi-kitchen"],["Indochine (restaurant)","dish/indochine-restaurant"],["Khô cá lóc","dish/kho-ca-loc"],["Lee's Sandwiches","dish/lee-s-sandwiches"],["Lúc Lắc Vietnamese Kitchen","dish/luc-lac-vietnamese-kitchen"],["Mắm (restaurant)","dish/mam-restaurant"],["Mắm nêm","dish/mam-nem"],["Mama Đút","dish/mama-ut"],["Matta (restaurant)","dish/matta-restaurant"],["Nước chấm","dish/nuoc-cham"],["Ốc vú nàng","dish/oc-vu-nang"],["Phở Hòa","dish/pho-hoa"],["Phở Kim","dish/pho-kim"],["Pho Oregon","dish/pho-oregon"],["Rose VL Deli","dish/rose-vl-deli"],["Shaking beef","dish/shaking-beef"],["Tamarind Tree (restaurant)","dish/tamarind-tree-restaurant"],["The Black Sheep (restaurant)","dish/the-black-sheep-restaurant"],["Tương","dish/tuong"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b4/Cha_ruoi.JPG/500px-Cha_ruoi.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Viethavvh at vi.wikipedia","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cha_ruoi.JPG"}},"ingredient/flatbread":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/flatbread","n":"Flatbread","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A flatbread is bread typically made with flour, water, and salt, with or without leavening, which are mixed and rolled into flattened dough. They are usually baked quickly at high temperatures and range from below one millimeter to a few centimeters in thickness. Flatbreads are distinguished from other flour-based foods such as loaf breads, which have lower proportions of crust relative to crumb; pancakes, which use liquid batter rather than firm dough; and crackers, which are dry and crisp. Flatbreads are the oldest and most common type of bread, and are often staple foods. Many types of flatbread are eaten globally."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-flatbread"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatbread"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",[["Doner kebab","dish/doner-kebab"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cd/Flatbread.JPG/500px-Flatbread.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Jonathunder","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flatbread.JPG"}},"ingredient/oregano":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/oregano","n":"Oregano","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Oregano, sometimes called wild marjoram, is a species of flowering plant in the mint family, Lamiaceae. It is native to the Mediterranean region but widely naturalised elsewhere in the temperate Northern Hemisphere, particularly the United States and Mexico."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-oregano"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregano"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",[["Salsa golf","dish/salsa-golf"]]],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine",[["Greek salad","dish/greek-salad"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Marinara sauce","dish/marinara-sauce"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Salchichón","dish/salchichon"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Edible salt","ingredient/edible-salt"],["Feta","ingredient/feta"],["Kalamata olive","ingredient/kalamata-olive"],["Olive","ingredient/olive"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Nutmeg","ingredient/nutmeg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Cumin","ingredient/cumin"],["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Pimento","ingredient/pimento"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/14/Origanum_vulgare_-_harilik_pune.jpg/500px-Origanum_vulgare_-_harilik_pune.jpg","w":500,"h":750,"by":"Ivar Leidus","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Origanum_vulgare_-_harilik_pune.jpg"}},"ingredient/urad-bean":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/urad-bean","n":"Urad (bean)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The black gram (Vigna mungo) is a bean grown in South Asia. Like its relative the mung bean, it has been reclassified from the genus Phaseolus to Vigna. The product sold as black gram is usually the whole bean, whereas the split bean (the interior being white) is called white lentil. It should not be confused with the much smaller true black lentil (Lens culinaris)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-urad-bean"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urad_(bean)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Idli","dish/idli"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Vigna mungo","ingredient/vigna-mungo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6f/Black_gram.jpg/500px-Black_gram.jpg","w":500,"h":355,"by":"Sanjay Acharya","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_gram.jpg"}},"ingredient/graham-crackers":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/graham-crackers","n":"Graham crackers","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 3 cuisines","intro":[["A graham cracker is a sweet cracker made with graham flour that originated in the United States in the mid-19th century, with commercial development from about 1880, and mass production beginning in 1898 by The National Biscuit Company. It is eaten as a snack food, usually honey- or cinnamon-flavored, and is used as an ingredient in some foods, e.g., in the graham cracker crust for cheesecakes and pies."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-graham-crackers"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graham_crackers"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Filipino cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Mindanao cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-mindanao",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]],["Visayan cuisine","cuisine/filipino-cuisine-visayas",[["Mango float","dish/mango-float"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Carabao mango","ingredient/carabao-mango"],["Condensed milk","ingredient/condensed-milk"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Ladyfinger (biscuit)","ingredient/ladyfinger-biscuit"],["Mango","ingredient/mango"],["Savoiarde PAT","ingredient/savoiarde-pat"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/87/Graham-Cracker-Stack.jpg/500px-Graham-Cracker-Stack.jpg","w":500,"h":359,"by":"Evan-Amos","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graham-Cracker-Stack.jpg"}},"dish/medovukha":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/medovukha","n":"Medovukha","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Medovukha is a Slavic honey-based alcoholic beverage. It is crafted by fermenting honey with water and is sometimes flavored with ingredients such as herbs or berries. While very similar to mead, medovukha is produced much faster (approx. less than 1 month of fermentation)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-medovukha"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Medovukha"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Ethanol","ingredient/ethanol"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Olga's Flavor Factory","ref":"site/olgasflavorfactory-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"olgasflavorfactory.com","note":"Russian-born home cook Olga's blog, with a dedicated Russian recipes category.","url":"https://www.olgasflavorfactory.com/category/russianrecipes/",